                    <article class="tc-card" id="eligibility">

                        <h2>3. Eligibility and Accounts</h2>

                        <p>
                            You must be at least 18 years old to create a Brandora account. By
                            registering, you confirm that you meet that requirement and that the
                            information you provide is accurate and kept up to date.
                        </p>

                        <h3>Account types</h3>
                        <p>
                            Accounts are created in one of two roles &mdash; brand or influencer
                            &mdash; and the role determines which features are available to you.
                            Brandora also operates separate administrator accounts used by our own
                            team to review profiles, process withdrawals, and handle disputes.
                        </p>

                        <h3>Registration and access</h3>
                        <ul>
                            <li>You must provide a valid email address, which is confirmed by a verification code before your account becomes usable.</li>
                            <li>New accounts are additionally subject to review before they gain full access to the platform.</li>
                            <li>Passwords must meet the minimum strength requirements shown at registration.</li>
                            <li>Repeated failed sign-in attempts will temporarily lock an account as a security measure.</li>
                        </ul>

                        <h3>Your responsibility</h3>
                        <p>
                            You are responsible for keeping your password confidential and for all
                            activity that takes place under your account. Tell us promptly at
                            <a href="mailto:care.brandora@gmail.com">care.brandora@gmail.com</a> if
                            you believe your account has been accessed without your permission. You
                            may not share an account, sell it, or transfer it to someone else.
                        </p>

                    </article>


                    <!-- 4 -->

                    <article class="tc-card" id="profiles-verification">

                        <h2>4. User Profiles and Verification</h2>

                        <p>
                            Your profile is what other users see. You are responsible for making sure
                            it is truthful and current.
                        </p>

                        <h3>Brand profiles</h3>
                        <p>
                            Brand profiles include your company name, the name of a contact person,
                            your industry, an optional website, an indicative monthly budget range,
                            and an optional logo or image. You confirm that you are authorised to
                            represent the company you register.
                        </p>

                        <h3>Influencer profiles</h3>
                        <p>
                            Influencer profiles include your name, your primary social platform and
                            username on it, your content niche, your audience size and follower
                            count, your engagement rate, and optional biography, location, website,
                            and rate notes. You confirm that the audience and engagement figures you
                            provide are genuine and not inflated by artificial means.
                        </p>

                        <h3>What verification means</h3>
                        <p>
                            Profiles carry a verification status and are reviewed by our team before
                            they appear in the public directories. A profile may be marked verified,
                            or rejected with a reason.
                        </p>

                        <div class="tc-note">
                            <p>
                                Verification means only that a member of our team has reviewed the
                                information submitted with a profile and considered it acceptable for
                                listing. It is <strong>not</strong> a guarantee of a user's identity,
                                honesty, audience authenticity, content quality, commercial
                                reliability, legal status, or results. It is not a background check,
                                an endorsement, or a warranty of any kind.
                            </p>
                            <p>
                                You remain responsible for your own due diligence before entering a
                                collaboration with another user.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <p>
                            We may change, suspend, or withdraw a verification status at any time
                            &mdash; for example if information turns out to be inaccurate or
                            circumstances change.
                        </p>

                    </article>


                    <!-- 5 -->

                    <article class="tc-card" id="using-the-platform">

                        <h2>5. Using the Brandora Platform</h2>

                        <p>
                            Subject to these terms, Brandora grants you a personal, non-exclusive,
                            non-transferable, revocable permission to access and use the platform for
                            its intended purpose.
                        </p>

                        <ul>
                            <li>Use the platform only for lawful purposes and in line with these terms.</li>
                            <li>Provide accurate information, and correct it when it changes.</li>
                            <li>Do not attempt to access data, accounts, or areas of the platform that are not yours.</li>
                            <li>Do not copy, scrape, or systematically extract content or user data from the platform.</li>
                            <li>Do not interfere with the operation or security of the service, or attempt to bypass any access control.</li>
                        </ul>

                        <p>
                            We may add, change, or remove features as the platform develops. Where a
                            change materially affects how you use Brandora, we will make a reasonable
                            effort to tell you.
                        </p>

                    </article>


                    <!-- 6 -->

                    <article class="tc-card" id="brands-influencers">

                        <h2>6. Brands, Influencers and Collaborations</h2>

                        <p>
                            Brands and influencers use the platform differently, and each carries its
                            own responsibilities.
                        </p>

                        <h3>If you are a brand</h3>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Describe campaigns honestly, including deliverables, budget, and deadlines.</li>
                            <li>Review submitted work within a reasonable time and either approve it or explain what needs changing.</li>
                            <li>Fund agreed milestones through the platform.</li>
                            <li>Do not ask influencers for work outside what has been agreed without agreeing it first.</li>
                        </ul>

                        <h3>If you are an influencer</h3>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Only accept work you can realistically deliver.</li>
                            <li>Deliver what was agreed, by the agreed date, and submit it through the platform for review.</li>
                            <li>Make sure your content complies with the law and with the rules of the social platform you publish on, including any requirement to disclose paid partnerships.</li>
                            <li>Do not misrepresent your audience, reach, or engagement.</li>
                        </ul>

                        <p>
                            Brands may shortlist influencers they are interested in. Only profiles that
                            have been approved appear in the public directories. Appearing in a
                            directory or a shortlist does not create any obligation on either side
                            until a proposal is accepted.
                        </p>

                    </article>


                    <!-- 7 -->

                    <article class="tc-card" id="campaigns-proposals">

                        <h2>7. Campaigns, Proposals and Agreements</h2>

                        <h3>Campaigns</h3>
                        <p>
                            A brand creates a campaign with a title, description, budget, and
                            optionally a platform, niche, target audience criteria, content
                            guidelines, start date, and deadline. A campaign may also be given a
                            milestone plan setting out the pieces of work it will be broken into.
                        </p>

                        <h3>Proposals</h3>
                        <p>
                            A proposal can be initiated by either side. It records a proposed amount,
                            the deliverables, an optional timeline, and an optional message, and it
                            remains pending until the receiving party accepts or declines it.
                        </p>

                        <h3>Collaborations and milestones</h3>
                        <p>
                            When a proposal is accepted, it becomes a collaboration. Work under a
                            collaboration is organised into milestones, each with its own title,
                            description, amount, and optional due date. For each milestone:
                        </p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>The influencer submits the work, with proof and any notes.</li>
                            <li>The brand reviews it and either approves it or requests a revision with a reason.</li>
                            <li>Payment for that milestone is released once it has been approved.</li>
                        </ul>

                        <div class="tc-note">
                            <p>
                                The agreement created when a proposal is accepted is between the
                                brand and the influencer. Brandora records it, provides the tools to
                                run it, and holds milestone funds &mdash; but is not a contracting
                                party to it and does not guarantee that either side will perform.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        @* ASSUMPTION: cancellation of an accepted collaboration is not modelled
                           as a distinct workflow in the codebase, so no cancellation rule is
                           stated here beyond pointing at the dispute process. *@

                        <p>
                            If a collaboration cannot be completed as agreed, the parties should first
                            try to resolve it between themselves through the platform. Where that is
                            not possible, the dispute process in section 12 is available.
                        </p>

                    </article>


                    <!-- 8 -->

                    @* ASSUMPTION: the platform fee rate is configurable
